Auloclasm

Ingredients: GABBER + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Woodwinds  
Creative Catalyst: It grew out of a secluded monastery where insomniac monks transcribe their collective night terrors into voltage-controlled modular synthesizer patches.

Auloclasm is the violent, breathless collision of 220-BPM distorted industrial kickdrums, spectral contrabass woodwinds, and unhinged analog oscillator screams. Damp monastic acoustic air clashes against blistering hard-dance pressure, turning sacred grief and sleep-paralysis tremors into an ecstatic, ritualistic rave.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 195–230 BPM; pulverizing four-on-the-floor gabber kicks saturated with diode clipping, overlaid with frantically hyperactive flutter-tongued woodwind flourishes, uneven triplet accents, and syncopated breath chuffs.
- Harmonic Palette: Dissonant microtonal Phrygian dominant, Ukrainian Dorian scales, quarter-tone reed inflections, and chilling suspended octave drones that refuse harmonic resolution.
- Production Signatures: Extreme asymmetrical distortion and tube drive applied directly to dry acoustic woodwind recordings; pitch-to-CV translation of recorded sleep-talking and rhythmic hyperventilation; cavernous plate reverbs instantly choked by brickwall sidechain compression keyed to the kick drum.
- Dynamics: Abrupt, suffocating drops from deafening, red-lined gabber blasts down to dead-silent rooms occupied only by the wet clicking of woodwind keypads and whispered chants, followed by terrifying, instantaneous rebuilds.

Scene & Origin Lore

Auloclasm was born in the waterlogged fens of East Anglia within the decaying conservatory of the Priory of St. Vitus-in-the-Fen. Faced with decades of chronic sleep paralysis caused by fen-gas exposure, the resident cloister began attaching piezos, EEG scalp electrodes, and throat microphones to their bodies while sleeping, routing the nocturnal voltage spikes directly into monolithic custom Eurorack synthesizers. By daylight, the novices attempted to soothe the machines by blowing into ancient bassoons, boxwood recorders, and hollowed elderberry pipes, only to discover that pairing overblown acoustic reeds with the modular systems' punishing sub-bass oscillators produced a trance state of ferocious catharsis. A feral underground youth culture—dubbed the "Novitiate Swarm"—soon annexed the priory’s abandoned glasshouses. Wearing soil-stained linen habits reinforced with reflective cycling straps, they gather at twilight on mossy flagstones to practice heelkloppen, an aggressive barefoot variation of the Dutch hakken dance designed to drive spirits back into the mud before sunrise.

Typical Instruments

- Contrabass Clarinet & Prepared Bassoon: Fitted with intra-bore contact microphones and run through analog overdrive pedals to produce gut-wrenching, seismic bass growls.
- Syrinx & Elderwood Fipples: Custom hand-carved wooden flutes played with violent overblowing techniques to produce shrieking multiphonics.
- Eurorack Modular Synthesizer: Buchla-style wavefolders, low-pass gates, and biofeedback CV modules translating dream-sleep EEG charts into synthesis parameters.
- Modified Roland TR-909: Pushed through vintage Germanium fuzz boxes and tape delays, tuned up to extreme tempos.
- Amplified Chime-Trees & Church Bells: Hammered with heavy wooden mallets to punctuate drops.

