Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Grafted Willow Chews the Wire
- Story & Context: During a record-breaking frost at St. Jude-in-the-Fen, Brother Klemens accidentally crossed the voltage lead of his dream-monitoring rig with the live wiring of an electric soil-heating grid buried beneath the conservatory's weeping willows. The sudden short-circuit translated his waking nightmare into a runaway 215-BPM kick pattern that blew the glass out of the eastern wall, while the frozen elderberry bassoons screamed with unprompted feedback. Rather than pulling the master breaker, the novices rushed into the freezing drafts, stomping their work clogs into the icy peat as Klemens howled into an overblown reed, "Splice the sleep into the root!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Broken conservatory windows looking out into a freezing fenland blizzard, glowing orange synthesizer patch cords entwined with bare weeping willow branches, frost crystals forming across analog vacuum tubes, cinematic grimdark realism, deep blues and amber tube glow.

Track 2: Low-Pass Filter for Dead Cotyledons
- Story & Context: Confined to the propagation cellar after refusing noon prayers, Sister Othelia spent forty sleepless hours routing the bioelectric resistance of dying orchid seedlings into her low-pass gates and contrabass clarinet. The subterranean room reeked of damp cedar dust and sour vinegar as she confessed her spiritual despair through a sequence of bone-rattling 225-BPM kicks that shook loose mortar from the cellar ceiling. When the Abbot pounded upon the bolted oak door demanding silence, Othelia pressed her throat mic against the woodwind's vibrating bell and roared through the keyhole, "The mold eats what the prayer forgets!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro artistic shot of dying green cotyledon sprouts pushing through black peat, backlit by the eerie red LED display of an analog Eurorack module, fine droplets of mist clinging to the wood grain of an antique bassoon bell, melancholic and claustrophobic aesthetic.

Track 3: Nachtkloppen in the Glass-Harrow
- Story & Context: Marsh wardens in the nearby village still whisper of the midnight gale that swallowed the entire novice congregation after an unauthorized, all-night dream-transcription ritual reached uncontrollable resonance. When searchers finally broke down the priory gates at dawn, they found the great glasshouse reduced to a mountain of powdered quartz and shredded vines, the air heavy with the smell of ozone and wet turf. No bodies were ever recovered, but pressed deep into the frozen mud were hundreds of bare heel-marks encircled by self-oscillating synthesizer towers, beside a final message carved with a wood-chisel: "Leave the marrow in the loam!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: The interior of a devastated glass palace at pale dawn, powdered glass sparkling like snow across muddy stone flagstones, thousands of deep heel prints circling an altar made of overgrown modular synthesizers and hollowed wooden pipes, painterly atmospheric lighting, haunting aftermath.

